at's now in  cambodia  is it a permanent occupying force are  they planning to leave at any time they  have talked this last spring about a  willingness to get out a willingness to  pull out and they said that they're  sending some troops home  and  uh i this is this is the sort of thing  that i was hoping to get into with the  leaders if i had met them or if i go  back  now and do meet the leaders that i want  to talk to them about to whatever extent  they'll talk to me about it and see what  their prognosis is  it's u.s policy to try to force them out  we would of course like to set up our  own government and  ironically we support paul pott  we were supporting pol pot when jimmy  carter was our president with a  humanitarian theme in his presidency and  he was butchering people so  the chinese also support pol pot but  john now the vietnamese been very  dependent upon the  russians for help  the russians also have a huge  hydroelectric  system and dams that they're  putting up supposed to be  operational about  1987 but i noticed in the paper that  some u.s businessmen are going over  there  and they're particularly interested in  agriculture in food distribution and oil  which is interesting because this is  what some of the radicals the anti-war  radicals were saying all along was the  primary interest of the united states  going into vietnam in the first place  huge deposits of offshore  oil  to date this oil has not materialized to  date  the the major corporations who could  help vietnam pump the oil are claiming  that the resources are not that easy to  tap  that they may have been exaggerated and  in fact vietnam has not been able to  bring in hundreds of millions of dollars  from oil  uh i my own guess is that in time they  will that in time  you know we've got a glut of oil in the  world today and the corporations are not  interested in dumping more oil into that  glut  my guess is that it's uh  it's a resource that's going to come in  10 or 20 years when we do in fact make a  bigger dent into the world's fuel  supplies  then vietnam will have its time of  wealth and there's another factor too  we're friends with china now and china  has asked american corporations to help  develop their oil and their oil is the  same oil as vietnam the oil doesn't stop  at the border it just continues on down  south indeed and and china of course is  claiming islands that have traditionally  belonged to vietnam and we're allied  with uh  of course you know as part of that our  alliance with china and the pressure  we're putting and realities harsh  realities of the world this is all why  vietnam is trying to open up  they they could they could you know  maintain uh  an island and keep people frozen out but  they're not going to keep up with the  rest of asia much less the rest of the  world unless they do find a way to get  into world trade  and this is what they're you know this  is why they're opening they're trying to  get tourism  john to conclude our discussion since  we're running out of time can you give  some final reflections  on your feelings going back to vietnam  10 years after  and of what u.s foreign policy should be  towards the vietnamese well a great  range of very powerful emotions i  i found myself getting very upset  depressed and upset in the first days it  was so beautiful and the people were so  nice and this this agrarian countryside  where the war has been erased so you're  looking at  rice fields and water buffalo  and friendly people everywhere you stop  the car friendly people smiling with  that incredible vietnamese warmth  and trying to figure out why anyone 10  000 miles away in my capital  would want to bomb the place much less  be able to rationalize doing so  the the diabolical madness of the people  that fly  37 000 you know a feet up in the air and  and b-52s and pull levers and punch  buttons and drop strings of bombs and  down in rice fields down below  and the people in the white house  kissinger plotting his maps and drawing  circles and fly here and bomb this and  yet the us itself hasn't done anything  to normalize relations with vietnam well  i was on my way to vietnam i read that  [Music]  first the cia director casey and then  schultz both stopped in bangkok and held  press conferences announcing that they  would  reopen covert activities against vietnam  working from thailand  it's pretty sick and when you're down  there on the ground thinking about that  i had trouble coping with it  and then also thinking the heat and  talking about health conditions and  thinking about the people that we  hired and drew into our working for the  us government and left behind and it  really struck me that you know those  people of course still in  re-indoctrination camps today we've  heard from some of them and some of them  have gotten out and others have  died  and  [Music]  i got i got upset i spent one day i was  i had meetings and things and i was  gonna  i got up they were supposed to start  about eight and i got up at six and went  out and i walked the entire  uh circumference of hanoi i walked 30 or  40 kilometers that day i went around the  city and zigged through it and around  the lake  uh crying much of the time just think  trying to cope with all of this and  think about it and it was very  depressing and eventually i worked my  way through it  great excitement and warmth  to to see the way the country has erased  the war and see the energy of the people  and the beauty of the place  and the friendliness of the place  [Music]  that and then my lease family is as  wonderful and warm as they were and that  was a surprise because i hadn't met more  than a couple i met a couple of her  sisters and her mother  uh  the the the handsome people  and so friendly and so nice that was  wonderful and the effects for jonathan  it was a trip that had a great range of  emotion  thank you for joining us tonight on  alternative views  we'd like to thank our directo